<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zongli Yamen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tsungli Yamen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zongli Yamen was the office in charge of foreign policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> in 1861 after the Convention of Peking. Before it, foreign relations had been handled by several agencies, among them the Ministry of Rites and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifan-yuan">Lifan Yuan</a>. It was supervised by five senior officials, at first all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, with Prince Gong as their leader in practice, and from 1875 it opened legations and consulates abroad. The Boxer Protocol of 1901 replaced it with a Foreign Office, the Waiwubu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen.jpg" alt="Zongli Yamen" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enrique Stanko Vráz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zongli_Yamen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Zongli Yamen is a traditional abbreviation of the office's official name, literally the Office in Charge of Affairs Concerning All Nations; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>, the other official language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, it was Geren gurun i baita be uherileme icihiyara yamun. The name is often taken to mean the Premier's Office, because zongli is now used for a premier or prime minister, but it named an office of foreign affairs; contemporary English sources called it the Board of Ministers for Foreign Affairs. Prince Gong established it on 11 March 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The office was the first significant institutional innovation in the central bureaucracy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> created the nucleus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> in 1729. Qing officials insisted that it would be temporary, kept only until the crisis of the time had passed. Its formal status was low, its members held posts in other agencies at the same time, and policy in foreign affairs remained the emperor's prerogative. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a>, set up to translate Western languages as successor to the Bureau of Translators, was placed under it rather than under the Hanlin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Within a few decades the office was overshadowed by powerful officials such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, but it remained the channel between the Qing government and the foreign ministers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>'s legation quarter. In 1873 it quarrelled with those ministers over the protocol of their audience with the Tongzhi Emperor, since they refused to perform the kowtow; the impasse was resolved partly through the Japanese ambassador, Soejima Taneomi, and similar protocol was followed for their audience with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> in 1891.</p><p>The missions it opened abroad were staffed by Qing diplomats with foreign staff and interpreters trained at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a>, and gave it some autonomy in presenting its own views in other capitals. Its successor under Article XII of the Boxer Protocol, the Waiwubu, ranked above the six boards, yet one judgement holds that it was as ineffective in establishing good relations with the outside world as the Zongli Yamen had been. The former site of the office, at No. 49 Dongtangzi Hutong in Dongcheng District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, lies in a historical conservation site, and nearly all its buildings are preserved in good condition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zongli Yamen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1867, when the <b>Zongli Yamen</b> proposed that young holders of examination degrees study mathematics and astronomy at the Tongwen Guan, the Grand Secretary Woren protested that the foundation of a state lay in propriety and righteousness, not in techniques, and that scholars should not take foreigners as…</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
